Package: merya
Type: Package
Title: Fast BCa Bootstrap Confidence Intervals and CI-Inversion P-Values
    for Common Statistical Tests and Models
Version: 0.7.0
Authors@R: person(given = "Your", family = "Name",
    email = "you@example.com", role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Drop-in replacements for stats::t.test(), stats::cor.test(),
    stats::lm(), and stats::glm() that report bias-corrected and
    accelerated (BCa) bootstrap confidence intervals and
    confidence-interval-inversion p-values instead of (or alongside) the
    classical asymptotic results. Functions take the same arguments and
    return objects of the same classes ("htest", "lm", "glm") as their
    original counterparts, so existing code and downstream methods keep
    working. boot.lm() can additionally report, for each predictor, its
    partial correlation, partial eta-squared, or eta-squared (with BCa
    confidence intervals and CI-inversion p-values) instead of its slope
    coefficient, and optionally a bootstrapped, leave-one-out predicted
    R-squared for the model as a whole. boot.glm() can report odds
    ratios, conditional or marginal (g-computation) risk ratios, marginal
    risk differences, or the population attributable fraction, in place
    of its raw coefficients. A single generic, boot.ci(), also takes an
    object already produced by t.test(), cor.test(), lm(), or glm() and
    returns it with the same BCa/CI-inversion inference substituted in,
    using the exact same internal computation as the corresponding
    boot.*() function. All bootstrap-based functions default to 10000
    resamples and to a fixed seed of 123 for reproducibility (settable to
    any other integer, or to NULL to use whatever random state is
    currently active instead of reseeding). The bootstrap resampling,
    BCa transform, and CI-inversion p-value machinery is implemented
    entirely in base R (using only the 'stats' and 'utils' packages that
    ship with every R installation, with no other dependencies);
    resampling is vectorized wherever a closed-form statistic allows it,
    and the BCa transform is inverted analytically for p-values (no
    bisection / root-finding), which keeps the bootstrap and p-value
    calculations fast even for large numbers of replicates.
